  "summary": "I wrote the reef scene in `src/` (`main.ts`, `common.ts`, `corals.ts`, `life.ts`). It passes `typecheck`. I couldn't run it or build it, and I couldn't view a rendered frame. The colours and light levels (tone mapping, fog density, caustic brightness) are my estimates and may need tuning. The shaders have never been compiled, so a GLSL error would only show up at runtime.\n\n- **Scene:** a bluish-green exponential fog gives the depth fade. The backdrop is a gradient sphere that is lighter toward the surface. A sun-angled directional light casts shadows, with a hemisphere fill light. Additive light shafts follow the sun direction.\n- **Seabed:** a 90×90 mesh with ripples over low dunes, coloured light to dark by ripple crest. Drifting caustic patterns are injected into the sand and coral materials.\n- **Corals:** five branching, four brain, four fan and three tube formations, plus rocks. About 16 more are scattered farther out, kept clear of the camera's orbit ring. The central branching coral sits on a rock.\n- **Plants:** instanced seaweed blades and eight anemones, all swaying in the vertex shader.\n- **Fish:** a school of 48 small fish moves along a shared Lissajous path with per-fish wobble. Four larger solitary fish swim their own elliptical paths. Tails wag in the shader.\n- **Particles:** 90 bubbles rise from five vents. Slow-drifting specks add depth.\n- **Camera:** it orbits the reef centre at a radius of about 13, with a slight height bob. It needs no input.\n- **Contract:** the app draws into `#app`, renders every frame with `requestAnimationFrame`, and sets `window.__EVAL_READY__` after the first frame.\n\n",
